Write darwin resolv stubs into the zig SDK.

zig's bundled macOS libc headers omit resolv.h. Go's net package and
net/internal/cgotest include it in every cgo build, so cross-compiling
any CGO program for darwin fails inside the Go standard library with
"fatal error: 'resolv.h' file not found".

The header stub satisfies both users. net/cgo_unix_cgo_res.go needs the
res_search declaration; cgotest compares sizeof(struct __res_state)
against Go's internal ResState ([69]uintptr, 552 bytes) and fails to
compile if the C struct is bigger, so the stub declares exactly that
size. The libresolv.tbd stub serves the link step; the companion
zig-wrapper patch points -lresolv at it. The dylib the stub names
exists on every macOS at run time.

Drop both patches when zig ships the header and a resolv stub.

diff --git a/toolchain/defs.bzl b/toolchain/defs.bzl
index 602a42d..09b90a6 100644
--- a/toolchain/defs.bzl
+++ b/toolchain/defs.bzl
@@ -274,6 +274,71 @@ def _zig_repository_impl(repository_ctx):
         sha256 = repository_ctx.attr.host_platform_sha256[exec_platform],
     )
 
+    # zig's macOS headers lack resolv.h, which Go's cgo stdlib includes.
+    # The stub declares res_search (resolved from libresolv at link time,
+    # only for programs importing net) and a struct __res_state of exactly
+    # 552 bytes, the size Go's internal ResState expects; cgotest fails to
+    # compile against a bigger one.
+    repository_ctx.file(
+        "lib/libc/include/any-macos-any/resolv.h",
+        "\n".join([
+            "/* Stub for zig cross-compilation to macOS; see",
+            "   hermetic_cc_toolchain-darwin-resolv.patch. */",
+            "#ifndef _RESOLV_H_",
+            "#define _RESOLV_H_",
+            "#include <sys/types.h>",
+            "struct __res_state {",
+            "  unsigned long __opaque[69];",
+            "};",
+            "typedef struct __res_state *res_state;",
+            "int res_search(const char *, int, int, unsigned char *, int);",
+            "#endif",
+            "",
+        ]),
+    )
+
+    # The linker half of the same gap: net's cgo LDFLAGS say -lresolv, and
+    # zig ships no stub for it. The wrapper rewrites the flag into the path
+    # of this stub (see the zig-wrapper.zig hunk); the dylib the stub names
+    # exists on every macOS at run time.
+    repository_ctx.file(
+        "lib/libc/darwin/libresolv.tbd",
+        "\n".join([
+            "--- !tapi-tbd",
+            "tbd-version:     4",
+            "targets:         [ x86_64-macos, arm64-macos ]",
+            "install-name:    '/usr/lib/libresolv.9.dylib'",
+            "exports:",
+            "  - targets:         [ x86_64-macos, arm64-macos ]",
+            "    symbols:         [ _res_9_init, _res_9_ninit, _res_9_nclose,",
+            "                       _res_9_nsearch, _res_9_search, _res_search ]",
+            "...",
+            "",
+        ]),
+    )
+
+    # Same trick for CoreFoundation, which Go's darwin runtime/cgo records
+    # as "-framework CoreFoundation". The framework exists on every macOS;
+    # the export list holds whichever symbols the Go runtime references.
+    repository_ctx.file(
+        "lib/libc/darwin/CoreFoundation.tbd",
+        "\n".join([
+            "--- !tapi-tbd",
+            "tbd-version:     4",
+            "targets:         [ x86_64-macos, arm64-macos ]",
+            "install-name:    '/System/Library/Frameworks/CoreFoundation.framework/Versions/A/CoreFoundation'",
+            "exports:",
+            "  - targets:         [ x86_64-macos, arm64-macos ]",
+            "    symbols:         [ _CFArrayGetCount, _CFArrayGetValueAtIndex, _CFDataAppendBytes,",
+            "                       _CFDataCreateMutable, _CFDataGetBytePtr, _CFDataGetLength,",
+            "                       _CFDictionaryGetValue, _CFEqual, _CFNumberGetValue, _CFRelease,",
+            "                       _CFRetain, _CFStringCreateWithBytes, _CFStringGetCString,",
+            "                       _CFStringGetLength, _kCFAllocatorDefault ]",
+            "...",
+            "",
+        ]),
+    )
+
     for target_config in target_structs():
         tool_path = zig_tool_path(exec_os).format(
             zig_tool = "c++",
